{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And each woman shall ask of her neighbor, etc.</b> There are commentators who explain that this asking is not the language of lending, for how could HaShem command to speak falsehood and to take by cunning? Rather, it is from the language of שְׁאַל וְאֶתְּנָה לָךְ spoken of King Solomon (1 Kings 3:5), which is an outright gift; and so too חַיִּים שָׁאַל מִמְּךָ נָתַתָּה לּוֹ (Psalms 21:5). Thus this asking means that they would give them gifts. And what is written (Exodus 12:36) וַיַּשְׁאִילוּם — that means that the Egyptians began to demand of them that they accept gifts from them, so much did they find favor in their eyes." "One must examine why here He mentioned vessels of silver and vessels of gold and garments, whereas in parashat Bo (Exodus 11:2), at the command, He did not mention garments, and afterward at the time of the deed He went back and mentioned garments there. And further, why did He say here וְשַׂמְתֶּם עַל בְּנֵיכֶם etc. — is it merely good advice He is conveying, what they should do with the garments? And why did He not give them advice about what to do with the vessels of silver and gold?\n\nAnd it appears to me that this asking took place only after the plague of the firstborn, after Pharaoh agreed to send them off, for before this why would they lend them? And the plague of the firstborn was like the onset of an epidemic, and there is a danger of taking garments from a house where there is a concern of corrupted air; therefore they did not need to ask the Egyptians to lend them their garments, for they themselves would give them the garments, for it was agreeable to them that they go into the desert wearing their garments in order that the wind would blow through them.\n\nAnd the Holy One, blessed be He, was concerned lest Israel be afraid to put on those garments because of the concern of the corrupted air within them; therefore He said וְשַׂמְתֶּם עַל בְּנֵיכֶם וּבְנוֹתֵיכֶם — and do not be afraid, for no evil shall touch them. Therefore in parashat Bo (Exodus 11:2), when HaShem commanded them to ask of the Egyptians, He did not mention garments, for HaShem knew that the Egyptians would lend them of their own accord. And perhaps it is on this account that it says וַיַּשְׁאִילוּם and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Berakhot 9a): against their will, that is, the Egyptians. And likewise it says (Exodus 12:35) at the time of the deed, that they asked also for garments — but not because of Israel's request; rather the Egyptians of their own accord lent them.\n\nAnd what is written here וְשָׁאֲלָה אִשָּׁה etc., where He also mentioned garments — this is not the place of a command but a telling of future events: that is to say, that thus it will be in the future, that they will lend them everything — part through Israel's request, namely the vessels of silver and gold, and part they will lend of their own accord, namely the garments." "And a hint to the matter: we find three instances of the word חֵן in connection with this asking — one in this parashah, two in parashat Bo — and three times חֵן yields the numerical value of silver [and] gold, to indicate that specifically the silver and gold they lent them was on account of the favor they found in the eyes of the Egyptians, but the garments were not included in the favor, for even if they had not found favor in the eyes of the Egyptians, they would have lent them, as stated.\n\nAnd with this, what is written here — וְשָׁאֲלָה אִשָּׁה מִשְּׁכֶנְתָּהּ, where He did not mention אִישׁ — is settled, whereas in the command it says וְיִשְׁאֲלוּ אִישׁ מֵאֵת רֵעֵהוּ (Exodus 11:2). This is because in the command He did not mention garments for the reason explained, and in the asking for vessels of silver and gold the men and the women were equal. But here, where He tells the future — that they would also ask for garments — and presumably the women whose husbands the firstborn had died would lend them, in order to spread the wind through the garments, therefore He mentioned only the women; and He speaks of the upper garment of the men, which the daughters too are accustomed to wear."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {1 "The phrase כְּדֵי שֶׁיָּפוּחַ הָרוּחַ בָּהֶם, literally 'in order that the wind would blow in them,' refers to the Egyptian hope that by having Israelites wear their garments in the desert, the wind would purify them of the suspected corrupted air; rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:24.295085643Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}